// Notes on the output layout
//
// Every line of the default output is made of:
//
//   <size> <tree drawing> <name><padding>│<bar> │ <percent>
//
// * The size column is right aligned. Its width is the widest size string of
//   all the displayed entries (19 characters when showing file times).
// * The tree drawing uses two characters per level of nesting followed by a
//   three character connector such as `├──` or `┌─┴`.
// * Names are padded so that all bars start in the same column. When the
//   terminal is too narrow the bars are dropped entirely and long names are
//   shortened and end with `..`.
// * The bar of a child is derived from the bar of its parent: blocks which
//   belonged to the parent are shaded according to the depth, and the blocks
//   representing the child itself are drawn solid.
// * The percentage is relative to the size of the root of the tree.
//
// By default the biggest entries are printed at the bottom, closest to the
// prompt; `--reverse` flips the tree so the root is printed first.
//
// The screen reader mode prints plain columns instead:
//
//   <name><padding> <depth> <size> <percent>
//
// Sizes
//
// * Disk usage is computed from the number of 512 byte blocks allocated to a
//   file. Hard links are only counted once unless apparent sizes are used.
// * With `--filecount` every regular file counts as one.
// * With `--filetime` the size of a directory is the most recent time found
//   below it.
//
// Selecting entries
//
// Entries are picked biggest first until the requested number of lines is
// reached; an entry can only be displayed when its parent is displayed. The
// depth limit, the minimum size, the collapsed directories and the
// directory / file only modes all restrict which entries may be picked.
//
// Errors
//
// Missing paths and directories which could not be read are reported once the
// walk is finished. When nothing at all can be displayed because the targets
// do not exist the program exits with a failure status.
//
// Threads
//
// The walk runs on a dedicated thread pool. Its stack size can be raised with
// `--stack-size` for very deep directory trees and the number of worker
// threads can be limited with `--threads`.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

'''
